        <aside aria-label="Author Bio" class="font-sans small-copy-size leading-small-copy-lh blue-link">
                                                                                    </aside>
        <aside aria-label="Translator Bio" class="font-sans small-copy-size leading-small-copy-lh blue-link">
                                                                        <div class="mx-auto mt-6 w-7 border-t border-black dark:border-white">&nbsp;</div>
                        <!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Wes Enzinna is <em>The Baffler</em>’s deputy editor. Previously, he worked as a senior editor at <em>Harper’s</em> magazine and a senior writer at <em>Mother Jones</em>. His longform reportage and essays have appeared on the covers of <em>The New York Times Magazine</em>, <em>Mother Jones</em>, <em>New York</em>, and <em>Harper’s</em>, where he is a contributing editor. He is finishing a book about a California homeless encampment for Knopf-Doubleday, for which he was awarded a 2022 Whiting Award for Creative Nonfiction.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
                                                                                <div class="mx-auto mt-6 w-7 border-t border-black dark:border-white">&nbsp;</div>
                        <p>Philip Connors is the author of four books, most recently <em>The Mountain Knows the Mountain</em>. His essays have appeared in the <em>New York Times</em>, the <em>London Review of Books</em>, <em>The Paris Review</em>, and <em>n+1</em>, among other places. He lives and works in the U.S.-Mexico borderlands.</p>
                                                                                <div class="mx-auto mt-6 w-7 border-t border-black dark:border-white">&nbsp;</div>
                        <p>Bertrand Cooper is an essayist and policy analyst living in Los Angeles. His writing has been featured in <em>Current Affairs</em>, the <em>New York Times</em>, <em>The Atlantic</em>, and <em>Dissent</em>. He hopes to finish his book proposal and write a book.</p>
                                                                                <div class="mx-auto mt-6 w-7 border-t border-black dark:border-white">&nbsp;</div>
                        <!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Sara Nović<strong> </strong>is the author of the novels <em>True Biz </em>and <em>Girl at War</em>. She has an MFA in fiction and literary translation from Columbia University, and is an instructor of Deaf Studies and creative writing. She lives in Philly.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
                                                                                <div class="mx-auto mt-6 w-7 border-t border-black dark:border-white">&nbsp;</div>
                        <p>Chris Rose is the author of <em>1 Dead in Attic: After Katrina</em>, and is a former reporter at the <em>Times-Picayune</em>, where he was a 2006 Pulitzer Prize finalist for Distinguished Commentary and was part of the newspaper’s team that won the 2006 Pulitzer for Public Service for their coverage of Hurricane Katrina.</p>
                                                                                <div class="mx-auto mt-6 w-7 border-t border-black dark:border-white">&nbsp;</div>
                        Bud Smith’s fiction has appeared at publications including <em>The Paris Review</em>, <em>Bomb</em>, and <em>The Nervous Breakdown</em>. He is the author of the novel <i>Teenager</i>, among others. He lives in Jersey City.
                                                                                <div class="mx-auto mt-6 w-7 border-t border-black dark:border-white">&nbsp;</div>
                        <p>Timmy Straw is a poet, musician, and translator from Russian. Their first book, <em>The Thomas Salto</em>, was published in 2023 by Fonograf Editions, and their poems appear in <em>The Paris Review</em>, <em>Harper’s</em>, <em>The Yale Review</em>, and elsewhere.</p>
                                                                                <div class="mx-auto mt-6 w-7 border-t border-black dark:border-white">&nbsp;</div>
                        <p>May Syeda is a writer living in Brooklyn.</p>
                                                        </aside>
